{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791302334,"nonce":"vWAapsIBt4iJMQw/C+UW0ju7iDc=","title":"Interpreted macro closures copy captured locals","message":"At aed0e5672dc4dd7fd347ec9053c76cb99d45eb55, Issue9147 reaches WRONG_ANSWER: its TypeTools.map callback pushes visited types into inMapper, but the outer array remains empty. TypeTools.map and anonymous definition views now work; mutable capture semantics are the remaining blocker.\n\nA standalone macro probe sorts visited types and calls a captured counter twice. Native Haxe prints Bool,Int / 1,2,2 and CONFORMANCE_OK. Rayzor's default tiered application preset prints an empty visited list followed by 1,1,0 and throws. Logs: /tmp/rayzor-macro-capture-haxe.log and /tmp/rayzor-macro-capture-before.log.\n\nEnvironment::capture_used snapshots MacroValue values, and call_function creates fresh bindings from those snapshots on every invocation. Mutations disappear on pop_scope. Capture the original lexical variable cells instead, preserving writes made by the closure or its caller, distinct invocations of enclosing functions, and escaped closure lifetimes. Copying mutations back by name would incorrectly write a shadowing variable in the caller's scope.\n\nMacroCapturedVariables.hx verifies callback array mutations, repeated counter calls, separate escaped counters, writes made by the outer scope after capture, and calls while a same-named local shadows the captured variable. Native Haxe produces CONFORMANCE_OK. The bytecode VM's function-value representation must accept the shared binding type; bytecode closure execution is not part of this interpreted-macro fix.","files":null}]}